at the degeneracy number of a graph G is deﬁned by\nD(G) = max\nH⊆G δ(H),\n(B.1)\nwhere the max runs over all subgraphs H of G, and δ(H) is the minimum degree of H.\nA graph G is said to be k-degenerate if D(G) ⩽k. Equivalently, G is k-degenerate if there\nexists an ordering x1, x2, . . . , xn of the vertex set of G such that\ndegG↾{x1,x2,...,xj}(xj) ⩽k\n(B.2)\nfor 1 ⩽j ⩽n, where G ↾{x1, x2, . . . , xj} denotes the induced subgraph.\nIt is an elementary fact [19, Theorem V.1] that all of the integer chromatic roots of\nG lie in the interval [0, D(G)], i.e., the chromatic number χ(G) is at most D(G) + 1. On\nthe other hand, Thomassen [115, Theorem 3.9] has shown that the real chromatic roots\ncannot be bounded in terms of the degeneracy number: indeed, there are 2-degenerate\ngraphs with arbitrarily large real chromatic roots. Here we shall use Proposition 2.2 and\nequation (2.32) to give a simple proof of the formula underlying (a slight generalization\nof) Thomassen’s construction, and to exhibit its exact range of applicability.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChromatic Root -- from Wolfram MathWorld (https://mathworld.wolfram.com/ChromaticRoot.html)\nciteturn8search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Tutte (1970) showed that Image: phi+1=phi^2 cannot be a chromatic root of any chromatic polynomial where, Image: phi is the golden ratio, a result extended to Image: phi^n for positive integer Image: n (Alikhani and Peng 2009). ... \"Chromatic Roots at 2 and the Beraha Number Image: B_(10).\"\n\n# Chromatic Root\n\n* * *\n\nDownload Wolfram Notebook\n\nA chromatic root is a root of a chromatic polynomial (Dong et al. 2005, Alikhani and Ghanbari 2024). Chromatic roots are potentially complex.\n\nTutte (1970) showed that Image: phi+1=phi^2 cannot be a chromatic root of any chromatic polynomial where, Image: phi is the golden ratio, a result extended to Image: phi^n for positive integer Image: n (Alikhani and Peng 2009).\n\nImage: HarveyRoyleGraphs\n\nIn contrast, Image: phi+2 is a possible chromatic root (Harvey and Royle 2020; e.g., the Harvey-Royle graphs depicted above), a result which can be extended to Image: phi+n for integer Image: n>=2 (Alikhani and Hasni 2012, Alikhani and Ghanbar 2024) using the result that if Image: alpha is a chromatic root, then for any natural number Image: n, Image: alpha+n is also a chromatic root.\n\nSokal (2004) showed that chromatic roots are dense in the complex plane (Cameron and Morgan 2016).\n\nImage: Chromatic roots on the real line and in the complex plane\n\nAn interval in which no chromatic roots are possible is known as a chromatic root-free interval. The plots above show a histogram of chromatic roots along the real axis and the positions of chromatic roots in the complex plane for graphs in `GraphData` (the latter of which shows clear deviations from uniformity).\n\n## See also\n\nChromatic Polynomial, Chromatic Root-Free Interval\n\n## References\n\nAlikhani, S. and Ghanbari, N. \"Golden Ratio in Graph Theory: A Survey.\" 9 Jul 2024. https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.15860.Alikhani, S., Hasni, R. \"Algebraic Integers as Chromatic and Domination Roots.\" Int. J. Combin., Article ID 780765, 2012.Alikhani, S. Peng, Y. H. \"Chromatic Zeros and the Golden Ratio.\" Appl. Anal. Disc. Math. 3, 120-122, 2009.Cameron, P. J. and Morgan, K. \"Algebraic Properties of Chromatic Roots.\" 3 Oct 2016. https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.00424.Dong, F. M., Koh, K. M.; and Teo, K. L. Chromatic Polynomials and Chromaticity of Graphs. Singapore: World Scientific, 2005.Harvey, D. J. and Royle, G. F. \"Chromatic Roots at 2 and the Beraha Number Image: B_(10).\" J. Graph Th. 95, 445-456, 2020.Sokal, A. D. \"Chromatic Roots Are Dense in the Whole Complex Plane.\" Combin. Probab. and Comput. 13, 221-261, 2004.Tutte, W. T. \"On Chromatic Polynomials and the Golden Ratio.\" J. Combin. Theory, Ser. B 9, 289-296, 1970.\n\n## Cite this as:\n\nWeisstein, Eric W. \"Chromatic Root.\" From MathWorld--A Wolfram Resource. https://mathworld.wolfram.com/ChromaticRoot.html--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRoots of the Chromatic Polynomial - Welcome to DTU Research Database (https://orbit.dtu.dk/en/publications/roots-of-the-chromatic-polynomial/)\nciteturn8search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; In particular we prove a tight lower bound on the smallest non-trivial chromatic root of a graph admitting a spanning tree with at most three leaves. ... In particular, we show that the chromatic roots of planar graphs are dense in the interval (3; 4), except for a small interval around _ + 2 _ 3:618, where _ denotes the golden ratio.\n\n# Roots of the Chromatic Polynomial\n\n  * Thomas Perrett\n\n  * Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science\n\nResearch output: Book/Report › Ph.D. thesis\n\n3986 Downloads (Orbit)\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe chromatic polynomial of a graph G is a univariate polynomial whose evaluation at any positive integer q enumerates the proper q-colourings of G. It was introduced in connection with the famous four colour theorem but has recently found other applications in the field of statistical physics. In this thesis we study the real roots of the chromatic polynomial, termed chromatic roots, and focus on how certain properties of a graph affect the location of its chromatic roots.\nFirstly, we investigate how the presence of a certain spanning tree in a graph affects its chromatic roots. In particular we prove a tight lower bound on the smallest non-trivial chromatic root of a graph admitting a spanning tree with at most three leaves. Here, non-trivial means different from 0 or 1. This extends a theorem of Thomassen on graphs with Hamiltonian paths. We also prove similar lower bounds on the chromatic roots of certain minor-closed families of graphs.\nLater, we study the Tutte polynomial of a graph, which contains the chromatic polynomial as a specialisation. We discuss a technique of Thomassen using which it is possible to deduce that the roots of the chromatic polynomial are dense in certain intervals. We extend Thomassen’s technique to the Tutte polynomial and as a consequence, deduce a density result for roots of the Tutte polynomial. This partially answers a conjecture of Jackson and Sokal.\nFinally, we refocus our attention on the chromatic polynomial and investigate the density of chromatic roots of several graph families. In particular, we show that the chromatic roots of planar graphs are dense in the interval (3; 4), except for a small interval around _ + 2 _ 3:618, where _ denotes the golden ratio. We also investigate the chromatic roots of related minor-closed classes of graphs and bipartite graphs.\n\nOriginal language  | English\nPlace of Publication  | Kgs. Lyngby\nPublisher  | Technical University of Denmark\nNumber of pages  | 115\nPublication status  | Published - 2017\n\nSeries  | DTU Compute PHD-2016\nNumber  | 438\nISSN  | 0909-3192\n\n## Access to Document\n\n  * Phd438 Perrett TFinal published version, 1.02 MB\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Alan D. Sokal (https://dblp.org/pid/65/595.html)\nciteturn8search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Sokal Image: 0000-0002-1891-0200: ... Theory B 91(2): 345-360 (2004) ... Bounds On The Complex Zeros Of (Di)Chromatic Polynomials And Potts-Model Partition Functions.\n      * details & citations\n \n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/cpc/Sokal01\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/cpc/Sokal01\n\nAlan D. Sokal:\nBounds On The Complex Zeros Of (Di)Chromatic Polynomials And Potts-Model Partition Functions. Comb. Probab. Comput. 10(1): 41-77 (2001)\n  * Image: Journal Articles\n\n[j1]\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n      * details & citations\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlan D. Sokal | University College London | 257 Publications | 3129 Citations | Related Authors (https://scispace.com/authors/alan-d-sokal-14nul06egl?papers_page=21)\nciteturn8search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Sokal is an academic researcher from University College London. The author has contributed to research in topics: Monte Carlo method & Critical exponent. ...  - arXiv: Computational Complexity ... TL;DR: A completely different, randomized polynomial-time algorithm based on Lovasz' theory of polymatroid matching for determining whether there exists a spanning hypertree in a given k-uniform hypergraph. ... Bounds on the Complex Zeros of (Di)Chromatic Polynomials and Potts-Model Partition Functions\n\nRenormalization Transformations: Source of Examples and Problems in Probability and Statistics\n\nAernout C.D. van Enter,Roberto Fernández,Alan D. Sokal +2 moreÉcole Polytechnique Fédérale de Lausanne,New York University\n\n- 01 Jan 1994\n\n[Input]\n\nTL;DR: In this paper, the authors review several interesting questions motivated by applications in physics as well as in other areas like image processing and speech recognition, and present several interesting answers motivated by physics applications.\n\n•Journal Article•10.1017/S0963548300004612\n\nBounds on the Complex Zeros of (Di)Chromatic Polynomials and Potts-Model Partition Functions\n\nAlan D. Sokal\n\n- 11 Apr 1999\n\n - arXiv: Statistical Mechanics \n\n[Input]\n\nTL;DR: For the special case of series-parallel graphs, this paper showed that the chromatic polynomial of the generalized Brown-Colbourn conjecture holds for all loopless graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGraph Coloring and Chromatic Polynomials — Mathematics · Fermi (https://beta.fermi.ai/math/graph-coloring-and-chromatic-polynomials)\nciteturn8search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; The real and complex zeros of chromatic polynomials (\"chromatic roots\") are far from understood. ...   * No chromatic root lies in the intervals (−∞,0), (0,1), or (1,32/27] — proven by Jackson and Thomassen for connected graphs. ...   * Computational reality: evaluating P(G,k) exactly is #P-hard, and even deciding χ(G)≤3 is NP-complete, so the elegant theory coexists with intrinsic computational intractability.\n## Subtleties & Limits\n\nOpen Problem\n\nWhere can the roots of P(G,k) live? The real and complex zeros of chromatic polynomials (\"chromatic roots\") are far from understood. Key facts and open questions:\n\n  * No chromatic root lies in the intervals (−∞,0), (0,1), or (1,32/27] — proven by Jackson and Thomassen for connected graphs.\n  * It is a theorem (Tutte) that P(G,k) has no roots in (1,32/27], but the distribution of complex roots is largely conjectural; they are dense in the complex plane for general graphs (Sokal, 2004).\n  * Birkhoff–Lewis conjecture: for planar graphs, P(G,k)>0 for all real k≥5 (known for k=5 and large k, open in between historically; resolved positively in key cases but the full real-line behavior remains delicate).\n  * Hadwiger's Conjecture (1943), among the deepest open problems in the field, asserts every graph with χ(G)≥t contains K t​ as a minor — proven for t≤6, open for t≥7.\n  * Computational reality: evaluating P(G,k) exactly is #P-hard, and even deciding χ(G)≤3 is NP-complete, so the elegant theory coexists with intrinsic computational intractability.\n\n## See Also\n\nBipartite Graphs · Chromatic Number and Independent Sets · The Tutte Polynomial · Planar Graphs and the Four Color Theorem · Brooks' Theorem · List Coloring and Choosability · Edge Coloring and Vizing's Theorem · Hadwiger's Conjecture · Computational Complexity and NP-Completeness :::--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImproved bounds on the zeros of the chromatic polynomial of graphs and claw-free graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.04366)\nciteturn8academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.5 years ago; Title: Improved bounds on the zeros of the chromatic polynomial of graphs and claw-free graphs ... We prove that for any graph $G$ the (complex) zeros of its chromatic polynomial, $\\chi_G(x)$, lie inside the disk centered at $0$ of radius $4.25 \\Delta(G)$, where $\\Delta(G)$ denote the maximum degree of $G$.\nTitle: Improved bounds on the zeros of the chromatic polynomial of graphs and claw-free graphs\nAuthors: Ferenc Bencs, Guus Regts\nDate: Wed May  7 12:34:30 2025\n\nWe prove that for any graph $G$ the (complex) zeros of its chromatic polynomial, $\\chi_G(x)$, lie inside the disk centered at $0$ of radius $4.25 \\Delta(G)$, where $\\Delta(G)$ denote the maximum degree of $G$. This improves on a recent result of Jenssen, Patel and the second author, who proved a bound of $5.94\\Delta(G)$. We moreover show that for graphs of sufficiently large girth we can replace $4.25$ by $3.60$ and for claw-free graphs we can replace $4.25$ by $3.81$. Our proofs build on the ideas developed by Jenssen, Patel and the second author, adding some new ideas. A key novel ingredient for claw-free graphs is to use a representation of the coefficients of the chromatic polynomial in terms of the number of certain partial acyclic orientations.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChromatic roots at 2 and at the Beraha number $B_{10}$ (https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.08397)\nciteturn8academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.3 years ago; Title: Chromatic roots at 2 and at the Beraha number $B_{10}$ ... First we exhibit graphs for which the Beraha n